WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.140
[United States Coast Guard Training Film.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.140
Nonclassified.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.140
Copyright 1944 by Paramount Pictures Inc.]

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.140
[The Inside Story]

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.140
[Foreword.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.140
The most misunderstood of all human ills are
those due to problems of the mind.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.140
People still react to many of the false ideas
of past ages.]

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.140
[There are, frankly, definite mental illnesses
of various sorts but the source of most mental

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.140
problems is simply emotional disturbance.]

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.140
[This causes as much if not more human pain
and suffering and maladjustment of the person

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.140
than does organic illness.]

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.140
[It is, of course, impossible to fully cover
this subject in any single book or picture,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.140
but this presentation, made for you,]

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.140
[endeavors to clarify the most common, fundamental
troubles that beset us as a result of emotional

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.140
upset.]

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.140
[A newborn baby is shown]

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.140
This is the beginning of Patrick Jones.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.140
From this time on, his life is going to be
a constant conflict.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.140
Reality, necessity, and duty; opposing desires,
dreams, and distractions.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.140
These conflicts exist in every human being,
and we must learn how to deal with them.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.120
Ten years later, he is still immature in thought
and action.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:25.560
Thinking, acting, Pat Jones, who can raise
more hell than any kid on the block.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.520
[Fire cracker explodes and his father yells
at him]

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:34.030
He has plenty of fun, some of it not exactly
in accord with adult ideas of peace and quiet,

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.730
but all in the routine pattern of boyhood.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.970
It makes him feel a little important to heckle
his father.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:47.640
Not in any spirit of mean-ness, but because
the old man represents authority and discipline.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.490
[Fire cracker explodes under a tin can]

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:57.190
Yes, over the years Dad has learned that fire
cracker shooting must be restricted at times.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.391
And this is going to hurt Dad more than it
does Pat.

00:01:59.391 --> 00:02:02.040
[Dad brings Pat inside the house]

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.400
As Pat grows older, he finds it pleasant to
have people make a fuss over him.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.730
Like the time when he won first prize in an
interscholastic contest.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:16.860
Or when he hurt his knee in the championship
football game and so covered himself with

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.269
glory that everyone made over and waited on
him.

00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:26.540
He was the four-star local hero and he loved
it, as who wouldn't.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.950
Inside himself, unconsciously, Pat will always
carry with him this memory.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.420
And Dad was proud as a peacock, he showed
the newspaper clippings until they were worn

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.080
out.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.410
This is home and it's great, friendly and
protective.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:48.090
Home means Mom's deep-dish apple pie, so good
Dad always had three pieces.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.950
And it's sitting around listening to the radio,
playing a little gin rummy with the old man.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.310
It's where he can call up Margie, whenever
he likes.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:02.730
Ask her to the movies, or to a dance, or just
down to the drugstore for a soda.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.540
Or if he feels like being alone, he can go
up to his own room.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:12.700
There's something mighty comfortable about
your own room.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.140
And he's a man on his own, too.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:19.129
Paying his own way, earning his own living.

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:25.440
Patrick Jones, junior foreman, holding down
a job, with a paycheck every Saturday, and

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.239
a good one for a guy of his age.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:28.790
Jones: Patrick R. Jones.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.069
Paymaster: Patrick R. Jones.

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:32.450
I see they're closing you out Pat?

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:33.450
Jones: Closing me out?

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.450
I quit.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:35.450
Paymaster: Bill Goss.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.450
Goss: Bill Goss.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:37.450
Sorry you're leavin', Jones.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.040
Jones: I'm getting a better job.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.040
So long.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:45.470
Narrator: And he could quit his job whenever
he liked, for whatever reason he liked.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:51.849
Overnight, his home life is left behind and
he's one of millions of men, each one a Seaman

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:53.620
Jones.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.549
That means he isn't necessarily our Seaman
Jones.

00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:03.129
This Seaman Jones may have graduated from
one of the ritziest colleges in New England.

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:09.460
Or he could be Seaman Rabinovitz, who never
made it beyond sixth grade of a public school.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:13.700
This one could've been one of the seven Swenson
kids from a farm.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.420
Or O'Brien, who never knew his folks and was
raised in an orphanage.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:25.020
But, whoever a man is you can tell a lot about
him by what's in his heart.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.460
Seaman Jones may be a little along in a couple
of years and have a wife and kids.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.090
Then again, he could just be a kid himself,
and still lookin' them over, if you know what

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.090
I mean.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:39.660
Or he could be the boy who's been going steady
with the girl who's always been his, and always

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:40.660
will be.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.229
[Take care of yourself.

00:04:42.229 --> 00:04:43.229
My darling]

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:47.050
So let's look at the inside story of this
Seaman Jones.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.830
Who's gone from this -- to this.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.729
[Patrick shown at his old job, to him in a
Coast Guard uniform]

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:55.720
From Mom's deep-dish apple pie to a mess line.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.840
From his own room, to this.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:07.130
[A full bunk dorm with men wrestling each
other]

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.820
Noise, confusion, no privacy.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:21.270
The 
change is a big one and he's having a hard

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.540
time adjusting himself to this new life.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.460
Seaman Jones is homesick, unhappy, and disturbed.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.610
Lots of things seem to be combining to worry
him.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.909
He feels that he is being unfairly neglected
by his family and his girl.

00:05:35.909 --> 00:05:37.839
He's had no mail for four days now.

00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:42.470
And it seems as though he's the only one who
misses.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.719
He's having trouble concentrating on his class
work.

00:05:46.719 --> 00:05:49.030
Doesn't seem able to follow.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:53.260
And he's the boy who used to win the silver
cups in high school.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:57.599
All-in-all, this is no picnic, says Seaman
Jones.

00:05:57.599 --> 00:06:04.849
He finds himself panicky and lost in a crowd
of countless men, where everybody looks alike

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:06.520
and is treated alike.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.890
No glory here like the time he was a football
hero.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.050
He's really worried about himself.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.290
So much so, that one night...

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:17.409
[Jones is sleeping in the bunk and talks in
his head]

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:19.659
Jones: Mustn't forget my general orders.

00:06:19.659 --> 00:06:21.529
[He hears yelling of orders]

00:06:21.529 --> 00:06:23.919
Jones: How will I pass inspection?

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:28.340
Will I get my liberty this weekend?

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.490
Are we going out tomorrow?

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.650
Will I get my stripes?

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.169
Exam coming up tomorrow.

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:40.330
Gee, I've got the jitters.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.849
Am I going nuts?

00:06:42.849 --> 00:06:46.010
Exams, drills, inspections, exams, drills
-

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.900
[Jones wakes up]

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:53.630
Jones: My God, maybe I'm going nuts; maybe
they'll drive me crazy!

00:06:53.630 --> 00:07:00.890
Narrator: Then, on top of all his other problems,
Seaman Jones, who'd always been a gay, friendly

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.099
guy, who got along with other people

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:08.890
found himself unable to enter into the general
easy companionship of the men with whom he

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:10.730
was living and working.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:12.910
[Men gambling]

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:18.651
Instead of joining in, he resented the sometimes
loud, rowdy fun in the barracks and nobody

00:07:18.651 --> 00:07:21.970
seemed to care how he felt.

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:28.770
Well, if all that isn't enough to trouble
one guy, all of the sudden the knee he twisted

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:32.870
in that grand and glorious football game went
back on him.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.740
It really hurt, plenty.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.790
But at least he can have that taken care of.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:43.450
And a mighty good doctor looks it over with
the greatest care.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:50.130
But the doctor can't find anything organically
wrong with the knee.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:55.620
Now the most startling thing about this upset
is that Seaman Jones isn't goldbricking.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:59.560
His knee really hurts and it's not imagination
either.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:03.660
He feels the pain, that's how he knows it's
there.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:09.420
That's important and it's frightening, too
because, on top of trouble with studies, and

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:12.529
nightmares, and loneliness, what can be wrong?

00:08:12.529 --> 00:08:14.050
He better do something about it.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.300
He does

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.660
the most sensible thing he could possibly
do: goes to the mental health department and

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.500
sees the psychiatrist.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:26.190
For naturally, an emotional upset must be
treated differently from an organic illness.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:30.430
Jones tells his story and the doctor is neither
surprised nor alarmed, for this isn't a new

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:33.419
story to him, amazing as it is to Jones.

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:35.110
And he knows the answer.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:39.490
An answer that Jones can understand because
there aren't a lot of strange highfaluting

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:40.490
terms.

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:47.380
Doctor: Today, we're making a scientific study
of mind and we're finding some very encouraging

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:48.890
things.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.170
Now, this nervous breakdown that you're afraid
of, for instance.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:57.070
Well, the truth is nobody has a nervous breakdown,
ever.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:58.950
Jones: They don't?

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:03.700
Doctor: Nerves, our physical nerves, don't
break.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:08.839
We do get into mental and emotional moods
that make us pretty unhappy, the results of

00:09:08.839 --> 00:09:11.860
all the difficulties and conflicts that beset
us,

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.720
that we've reacted to, that we've had difficulty
adjusting to.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.120
Now, your symptoms suggest that you are in
such a mood.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.750
A mood of anxiousness.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:28.610
Even your physical symptoms, like the aching
knee you told me about, can have an emotional

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:29.610
cause.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.130
I'll explain that in a moment.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.770
Anxiousness is quite a common mood.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:39.150
It makes a person focus his attention much
more closely on himself.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:43.880
Perhaps he notices his heart beating or his
muscles tensing.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.120
Jones: Yes.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.650
Doctor: People often think in this mood of
anxiousness that it's leading to a mental

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:49.940
illness.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:53.120
Actually, it almost never does.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.260
Perhaps I can help you if I give you an idea
of how your mind works.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:06.030
You see, Jones, emotions actually can cause
real physical pain.

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:11.620
The conscious mind is a pleasant sunny place
where normal thoughts have free reign.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:16.020
The unconscious mind is dark and mysterious
and harbors such thoughts as:

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.740
"I'm lonesome.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.360
Nobody pays any attention to me.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.040
I wish I were dead.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.620
I must be losing my mind."

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:28.470
These thoughts are constantly trying to get
to the conscious mind to get past the censor

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:29.540
who polices the band.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.010
[Cartoon of characters scaling wall guarded
by censor] It's his job to keep them out.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:33.190
[A walnut was thrown at the censor's head]

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.190
Censor: Hey!

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:43.450
Doctor: Here's a little thought that keeps
popping up.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:48.712
[Walnut turns into a metaphoric thought]

00:10:48.712 --> 00:10:49.712
Walnut-boy: I'm lonesome.

00:10:49.712 --> 00:10:50.712
[Censor throws thought back to unconscious
side]

00:10:50.712 --> 00:10:51.712
Suicide man: I wished I was dead.

00:10:51.712 --> 00:10:52.712
Censor: Huh?

00:10:52.712 --> 00:10:53.780
What did you say?

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:57.690
Suicide man: I said I wished I was dead.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:06.740
[This thought shoots himself in the head with
a toy gun]

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:10.500
Blind man: Uh, I must be stupid; I just can't
see things clearly.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:16.270
[Blind man falls over the wall into conscious
mind]

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:23.400
Censor: Outside, bums!

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:30.190
Two-headed man: I'm my own worst enemy!

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:37.060
Fearful man: Stop pickin' on me!

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:42.780
Someone's always pickin' on me!

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.900
Unicyclist: Nobody pays any attention to me.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:47.780
They're blind to real talent!

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:49.590
Huh, watch this.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:58.030
[Unicyclist cycles very fast but runs over
someone and his head is blown off]

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:02.170
Man who got run over: I must be losing my
mind!

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:03.170
Censor: Holy cats!

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:04.520
I must be losing mine!

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:14.442
[Censor catches all of the thoughts and sends
them back to the unconscious side of the wall]

00:12:14.442 --> 00:12:18.710
Censored out of the conscious mind, these
self-pitying thoughts tumble into the lap

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:20.880
of Old Unconscious Mind,

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:26.080
the dormant parent of all your emotions, and
arouse him with their clamor.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:30.120
[A sleeping giant is woken up by the thoughts]

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:35.390
They goad him to find another way for them
to assert themselves on your conscious mind.

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:41.690
So, this brute, who never forgets anything
that ever happened to you, remembers a certain

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:42.690
incident.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:43.860
Unconscious Mind: Hmm ... hmm ...

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:49.090
[Flashback of when Jones hurt his knee playing
in the football championship]

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:53.110
Which got you a nice lot of sympathetic attention
in the past.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:57.700
An incident which your conscious mind may
have completely forgotten, but your unconscious

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:00.710
remembers and now he knows just what to do.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.480
Unconscious Mind: I've got it, boys!

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:03.960
Into the car with you!

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.118
[Excited talking amongst the self-pitying
thoughts as they get into the car]

00:13:05.118 --> 00:13:06.118
[Switches: Nervous System.

00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:07.118
Transportation Co.

00:13:07.118 --> 00:13:08.118
Arm.

00:13:08.118 --> 00:13:09.118
Neck.

00:13:09.118 --> 00:13:10.118
Eye.

00:13:10.118 --> 00:13:11.118
Pelvis.

00:13:11.118 --> 00:13:12.118
Head.

00:13:12.118 --> 00:13:13.118
Ear.

00:13:13.118 --> 00:13:14.118
Feet.

00:13:14.118 --> 00:13:15.118
Knee]

00:13:15.118 --> 00:13:16.118
[Unconscious Mind pushes the car into a tunnel
and flips the switch labeled knee]

00:13:16.118 --> 00:13:17.118
[Car travels down the spine to the knee]

00:13:17.118 --> 00:13:23.880
[Thoughts come out of car with demolishing
tools and begin to hit the knee]

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:31.180
Two-Headed man: Why didn't we think of this
before?

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.090
Walnut boy: I'm not lonesome anymore!

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:43.560
Man on jackhammer: I've got some attention
now, this'll really make him hurt!

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:48.160
Doctor: And that's why your knee hurts, even
though there's nothing physically wrong with

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.160
it.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.220
Jones: Well, I'm sure glad to hear that.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:53.910
Doctor: A lot of boys feel about the way you
do.

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:57.160
There's another seaman in here a few days
ago named Scott.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:03.680
A big strapping lad, who during his civilian
days never had anything wrong with him.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:07.770
He won golden glove tournaments and was a
very pretty fighter indeed.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:15.820
[Flashback of Scott knocking out the other
fighter and winning the match]

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:21.500
Doctor: Then he landed in the Coast Guard
and found himself no more important than any

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:22.800
other seaman.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:27.820
He found himself being ordered around by a
Petty Officer half his size; being told what

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:35.230
to do and how to do it.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:40.380
Having his hat straightened for him and even
having the lint dusted off his clothes.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:46.770
He, a champion, in his own right.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:53.170
And even in the classroom, he found himself
thrown for a loss by simple navigation.

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:56.410
He just couldn't turn out an adequate notebook.

00:14:56.410 --> 00:15:00.071
So, one day he turned up at sick bay with
a bad stomach ache.

00:15:00.071 --> 00:15:09.770
A 
physical exam, x-ray, and laboratory studies

00:15:09.770 --> 00:15:15.790
proved there was nothing physically wrong,
so he was sent to the psychiatrist.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.339
What went on in his mind was something like
this ...

00:15:18.339 --> 00:15:23.120
He had a lot of self-pitying thoughts running
around in his mind for all his toughness.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:26.210
And of course, he wouldn't let his conscious
mind think that.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:34.830
[Censor catches self-pitying thoughts and
throws them back to the unconscious mind side]

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:42.090
Doctor: The censor chases them into his unconscious
mind and Old Man Unconscious decides what

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.300
Seaman Scott needs

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:47.930
is a darn good foolproof excuse for not doing
so well right now.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.640
And he takes care of that in his own way.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.330
[Self-pitying thoughts get into car]

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:54.950
[Switches: Nervous System.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:56.029
Transportation Co.

00:15:56.029 --> 00:15:57.029
Arm.

00:15:57.029 --> 00:15:58.029
Neck.

00:15:58.029 --> 00:15:59.029
Eye.

00:15:59.029 --> 00:16:00.029
Pelvis.

00:16:00.029 --> 00:16:01.029
Head.

00:16:01.029 --> 00:16:02.029
Ear.

00:16:02.029 --> 00:16:03.029
Feet.

00:16:03.029 --> 00:16:04.029
Stomach]

00:16:04.029 --> 00:16:11.620
[Unconscious Mind pushes the car into a tunnel
and flips the switch labeled stomach]

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:13.480
[Car travels down to the stomach]

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:15.339
Jackhammer man: Hey fellas come on!

00:16:15.339 --> 00:16:16.890
Let's get off of there!

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:17.890
Get to work!

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:18.890
Come on!

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.850
[Thoughts come out of car with demolishing
tools and chat excitedly]

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:30.220
Fearful man: They're always pickin' on me,
now it's my turn to do the picking!

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:34.930
Doctor: Everybody has emotional disturbances
and nobody is really strong enough to control

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.670
his emotions absolutely.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:39.620
I have some notes here that I'll give you.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:41.870
Look them over.

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:45.420
They may clear up some of the things that
have been bothering you.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.540
Jones: Thank you, sir.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:54.330
Narrator: So, Seaman Jones did study the notes
and found them even more helpful than he'd

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:55.330
hoped.

00:16:55.330 --> 00:17:00.800
They were interesting, understandable, and
they made sense.

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:03.860
[Mental Health]

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:07.029
[Inside of us, there is always a battle going
on between what we want to do, and what we

00:17:07.029 --> 00:17:08.029
have got to do...]

00:17:08.029 --> 00:17:09.029
Seaman: Get the professor, would ya?

00:17:09.029 --> 00:17:11.170
Jones: Some military doctor gave me, how to
go nuts in three easy lessons, or something.

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:13.750
Kelley: Hey, you ought to give it to some
of my Petty Officers.

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:16.730
I've got a guy over me that's a real Frankenstein.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:18.500
You know it worries me.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:20.900
Jones: Oh yeah, well, it says here that:

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:24.829
"Maybe you're blaming your Petty Officer because
you have a sneaking feeling that you can't

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:26.909
keep up with the rest of the fellas."

00:17:26.909 --> 00:17:29.210
Kelley: You mean it's me that's nuts?

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:30.809
Jones: Everybody does funny things.

00:17:30.809 --> 00:17:32.370
Listen to this:

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:36.710
"Inside of us, there is always a battle going
on between what we want to do, and what we've

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:38.029
got to do."

00:17:38.029 --> 00:17:42.862
Kelley: Yeah, that's the way I feel alright.

00:17:42.862 --> 00:17:47.230
Somehow, whenever I get a dirty job to do,
no matter what kind, maybe like swabbing down

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:50.020
the deck, I can't seem to think straight.

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:54.940
All of the sudden I ain't Seaman Kelley no
more, I'm somebody else.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:18:01.370
I look at all that deck that's gotta be swabbed,
and ugh.

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:07.610
Narrator: And his mind is a million miles
away as he dreams himself into an out-of-the-world

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:09.130
hero.

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:14.380
This time as a spy in Hirohito's palace.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:19.010
A sort of sea-going Dick Tracy, eh Kelley?

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:21.120
[Kelley lifts up part of his shirt to reveal
a button]

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:26.510
[Secret Operator 22 1/2]

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:33.240
[Kelley continues swabbing the floor]

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:41.320
[Palace doors open to reveal Hirohito]

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:52.100
[Kelley pulls out a gun from his pants]

00:18:52.100 --> 00:19:01.530
[Kelley goes after Hirohito with a gun]

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:05.570
[Gunshots are heard]

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:12.150
Of course there has to be a reward for a daring
deed like that.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:13.150
[Cheering]

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:18.430
And it's a real double barreled homecoming
for, yup, Admiral Kelly, right down Fifth

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:19.430
Avenue.

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:20.840
Crowd: Yay, Kelley!

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:22.640
Come on, Kelley!

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:26.830
And this is so real he can hear the crowd
yelling his name.

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:29.400
Petty officer: Kelley!

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:30.400
Kelley!

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:31.400
Kelley!

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:33.240
Get to work!

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:35.780
Kelley: Yeah, sure.

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:37.480
Sure, sure.

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:39.520
That's why I can't stand that Petty Officer.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:43.040
Jones: Well, is it because he's a bad egg,
or just because he had to pull you back to

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:44.040
reality?

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:45.460
Kelley: No, don't get me in no argument, son.

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:47.520
Well, all right, maybe it was.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:51.210
Come on, give out with more of our problems,
Mr. Antony.

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:54.960
Jones: Well, maybe some of you guys don't
like these man-to-man combat drills.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:56.290
Seaman: I always did hate them.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:20:00.350
Kelley: Well look, you don't need to be no
sawbones to figure that one out.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:03.140
You got...ah...inferiority dingus.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:08.280
Yea, it's like what they call an inferiority
complication.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:13.530
Jones: It says here: "When you have a feeling
of inadequacy, when you feel you can't keep

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:14.620
up with the competition,"

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:19.020
"you may begin to blame the task you're having
trouble with, instead of realizing it is your

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:20.800
own weakness that is troubling you."

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.760
Seaman: Well, does it tell me how I can get
cured?

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:25.990
Second seaman: Well sure, you've got to know
something about it.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:27.850
Now everyone has inferiority.

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:30.440
It's not that that's bad.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:34.300
Inferiority helps us a lot of times because
we try to improve ourselves.

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:39.490
Now if we do something that turns out good,
that helps us, but there's always inferiority

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:40.490
in all people.

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:42.170
Seaman: Yeah, but that's just horse sense.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:46.880
Jones: The doc says, "Mental health is just
applying common sense to your emotional problems."

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:51.500
Kelley: Sure, only books got to put it in
fancy words, so the guy who wrote it can get

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:53.390
three bucks a copy for it.

00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:57.490
Jones: Hey, get this: "It is hard to recognize
jealousy in oneself."

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:02.470
"For example, most of us secretly wish we
could be All-American athletes.

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:07.370
"Because we can't, we like to say athletes
are dumbbells, all brawn and no brains."

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:11.290
Seaman: And that, my fellow scientist, is
what is known as the sour grapes complex.

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:14.160
Second seaman: That's maybe why we say most
beautiful girls are dumb.

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:16.150
Kelley: You think that's bad?

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:20.910
Jones: No, but if we know what it is, we can
use it to help us to get ahead.

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:23.580
Seaman: Say, where did you get that fortune-telling
bunch of notes anyway?

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.490
Jones: The doc gave it to me.

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:30.540
A couple of weeks ago I started to get ... Well,
I thought my nerves would snap.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:31.600
Seems they can't snap.

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:34.450
Your nervous system is just like anything
else.

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:40.500
It gets tired and upset, but a good night's
sleep, or a little play and relaxation, it's

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:41.660
okay again.

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:45.500
Nerves don't break; it's our feelings that
get the best of us.

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:46.550
Seaman: Say, no kidding.

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:47.770
You know what I'm afraid of?

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:49.580
Second seaman: What, work?

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:52.380
Seaman: No, I'm afraid of being a coward.

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.600
Second seaman: Gee, me too.

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:57.650
Seaman: No, sometimes I can't think of anything
else.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:04.930
I know you'll think I'm a dope or something,
but sometimes I go to chapel and I sit there,

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:06.130
and I start sweating.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:10.030
Seaman: And I say, "Keep me from being a coward.

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:11.030
Oh God,

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:17.910
"what am I going to do if I get into action
somewhere and I get so scared I turn traitor?"

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:27.630
Narrator: That's a natural terrible fear,
the fear of doing this: running away.

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:30.130
A fear we all have.

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:32.900
Yet it's a normal fear.

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:35.300
For it's deep-rooted in every animal.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.380
We call it the instinct of self-preservation.

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:46.910
Animals react to it instinctively in order
to protect themselves.

00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:52.320
So we know that we may be afraid, but fear
and cowardice are not the same.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:56.320
In fact, fear is an emotion which has always
protected us.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:01.299
For example, here's an American submarine
cruising quietly.

00:23:01.299 --> 00:23:06.080
Suddenly, an enemy bomb crashes into it.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:11.800
It doesn't explode, but it's live, and it
can any second, and it's jammed in an almost

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.590
inaccessible place below.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:30.610
Only this skinny boy can get at it.

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:34.760
He must remove the detonator even though he
doesn't know how.

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:36.480
He's scared blue.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:40.549
He can be guided only by instructions from
a loud speaker.

00:23:40.549 --> 00:23:43.370
But here's a great thing about fear.

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:48.170
Sometimes, in this case for instance, it can
actually help.

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:53.330
For it causes an instantaneous change to occur
in our bodies so that every muscle and organ

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:55.190
goes into high gear,

00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:58.710
to help us through the emergency, as it's
helping this seaman,

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:03.620
while he's literally got the sub and the lives
of the crew in his hands.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:07.990
Stimulated by fear, the heart pumps faster
to carry more blood through the arteries.

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:12.460
The glands step up their secretions of vitalizing
fluids into the system.

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:18.710
And he's doing what he has to do, as frightened
as you will ever be.

00:24:18.710 --> 00:24:22.860
Fear is acting helpfully to heighten all responses.

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:27.820
Waste materials which might slow us down are
quickly thrown off and there comes an amazing

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:32.059
intensity of concentration to carry us through
emergencies.

00:24:32.059 --> 00:24:36.640
You can see the terror in this man's face,

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:41.750
but by stepping up his physical reactions
to help him through the most dangerous crisis

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:47.430
of his life, fear has become his ally instead
of his enemy.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:51.799
Aided by fear, he's done it.

00:24:51.799 --> 00:24:57.690
Now Seaman Jones, and the rest of his mates
who were troubled, understand more fully some

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:00.260
of the sources and causes of their worries.

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:05.330
And as a result, Jones is no longer lonely
and out of things.

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:07.270
He's one of the guys.

00:25:07.270 --> 00:25:11.220
He's learned what his mistakes were and corrected
some of them.

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:15.669
He's adjusted to his new environment and he's
doing all right.

00:25:15.669 --> 00:25:17.400
The same is true of Seaman Scott.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:21.620
He's all straightened out now and is doing
a swell job.

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:25.310
He even thinks of those bugaboo Petty Officers
as good guys now.

00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:28.490
And this one's a swell ping pong player, too.

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:33.350
Scott has finally found the right outlets
for his energy and ambition.

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:37.350
And Kelly the dreamer, he's not dreaming now.

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:42.309
He's having a good time and he's studying
hard, for he's learned that it's only through

00:25:42.309 --> 00:25:46.929
our own efforts that we achieve real satisfaction.

00:25:46.929 --> 00:25:56.510
Yes, all people must learn to live and work
in harmony, must assume new responsibilities

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.140
and take pride in their duties.

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:04.740
That's what is now reflected in these men's
faces as they get combat orders.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:08.870
Anderson looks confident.

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:11.270
Kelly is alert and ready.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:17.080
Scott is cool, steady warrior, and Seaman
Jones is going after new recognition.

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:22.809
So then, we see how trouble is stayed, as
we learn even a little about our minds,

00:26:22.809 --> 00:26:27.430
and thereby protect ourselves against some
of the unconscious trends to which we are

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:29.049
subject.

00:26:29.049 --> 00:26:35.630
Sound mental health is our nation's greatest
asset, and this we must maintain.

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:37.230
With it, let us have pride.

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:41.410
Pride in our corps, and in our service.

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:50.140
And we must have faith and hope, in our cause,
our way of life, and for a better world tomorrow.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:51.140
[The End.

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:52.140
MG-4068.

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:52.141
1944]

